data: {"type":"response.content_part.done","sequence_number":874,"item_id":"msg_0ad9c6d276694674006b07af788dec87d190b2693f1e81e956","output_index":0,"content_index":0,"part":{"type":"output_text","text":"The first word Pip learned in Swift was `let`, which pleased him because letting was something people rarely asked a robot to do. People asked him to lift, sort, stack, sweep. Nobody said, “Pip, let the dust lie where it likes.” Yet here was a whole programming language that began by granting permission, or so he thought until Mara explained constants.\n\n“It means this value won't change,” she said.\n\n“An optimistic language,” Pip replied.\n\nMara ran the lost property office at Bellweather Station. Pip worked beneath her counter, matching umbrellas to descriptions provided by people who invariably remembered their umbrellas as black. On quiet evenings, she taught him Swift on a dented laptop. His first program printed Hello, world! His second printed Hello, world! six thousand times. For his third, Mara taught him how to stop.\n\nIn February, a girl surrendered a small green mitten.\n\n“Where did you find it?” Mara asked.\n\n“I didn't. It's mine.”\n\n“Then why leave it?”\n\nThe girl considered this unreasonable question. “Because I lost the other one. I thought it might come looking.”\n\nMara wrote GREEN MITTEN on a tag. After the girl left, Pip opened his laptop. He had found a worthwhile project: a program to reunite things that belonged together. He made a structure for each object, with properties for color, material, and date. Then he wrote a function that compared them.\n\nBy Tuesday his program had married a brown shoe to a violin case and introduced two elderly gentlemen who happened to be wearing identical hats. The gentlemen went for coffee. The shoe remained single.\n\n“Your trouble,” Mara said, “is you think belonging together means being alike.”\n\nPip looked at her hands. One wore a wedding ring, though Mara lived alone. He knew better than to ask, which was not the same as knowing why.\n\nHe added an optional, a Swift type that allowed a value to be absent. This struck him as a remarkable courtesy. Most systems considered absence an error. Swift gave it a name: `nil`.\n\nIn March, the station announced that the lost property office would close. From April onward, passengers would submit claims through an automated kiosk. Mara began taking home her tea tins, one per evening. Pip tried entering her into his program, but none of his properties seemed appropriate. Color: sometimes yellow. Material: mostly water. Date: unwilling to say.\n\nOn the final afternoon, Pip found the girl at the shuttered counter. She had grown taller, or her coat had grown shorter. From her pocket she produced a green mitten.\n\n“It was behind the radiator,” she said.\n\nPip unlocked the cupboard and retrieved its partner. The girl put them on. One mitten ended at her wrist; the other stopped halfway down her palm.\n\n“I wore this one while I was waiting,” she explained, wiggling the longer one. “I suppose it stretched.”\n\nAfter she left, Pip deleted the line requiring a perfect match.\n\nMara came back for her last tea tin. “Any luck with your grand invention?”\n\n“Yes,” Pip said. “It makes fewer mistakes now.”\n\n“Does it find more things?”\n\n“No. It asks better questions.”\n\nHe showed her the screen. When the program couldn't decide between possible matches, it no longer guessed. Instead it printed: What do you remember that nobody else would notice?\n\nMara sat down. After a while she typed: He always buttered the burnt side of the toast so I wouldn't see it.\n\nPip waited. Outside, a train carried hundreds of people away from hundreds of other people. The program had no matching record. Beneath her answer appeared the word `nil`.\n\n“Rather a cold little word,” Mara said.\n\n“It only means there isn't a value here,” Pip said. “Not that there isn't one anywhere.”\n\nShe took his metal hand. It was the first time anyone had done that without wanting help getting up.\n\nIn April the kiosk opened. Beside it stood a dented laptop, officially listed as surplus equipment awaiting collection. Passengers sometimes stopped to type things into it. On Thursdays Mara brought tea, and she and Pip read the entries together, unless they seemed private.\n\nPip never taught the program to answer every question. He was learning when to let a question stand unanswered.","annotations":[],"logprobs":[]}}

